#### Sidecars / helper containers

Implementing infrastructure related changes to code-base raises serveral issues, like:

- Repeat the same logging logic across all services, including Auth-service, User-service, Order-service, and Payment-service, would increase LOE.
- A Bug in logging code can crash the main application.
- Updating to logger requires re-deploying the main app.
- Tight-coupling to the logger. Moving to different logger requires great LOE.

Instead of writing logging code, we could create container and attaches it to every services. This would create lossly coupling.

#### Kublet

Kublet is an agent that runs on every worker node. It's the middle man between the Control Panel and the actual containers on that Worker Node.

Kublet's responsibilities:

- Gets Pod's specifications
- Maintains the container's lifecycle
- Run probes like liveness, rediness etc

#### CRI (Container Runtime Interface)

CRI is the standard gRPC interface that decouples Kublet from any specific container runtime, allowing runtimes like containerD or Docker shim to be swapped without changing Kublet code.

- Here, Kublet send a request to CRI, CRI will call comments such as pull image, create container, start container or kill container.
- CRI runtimes can be Docker shim, ContainerD etc.

**CRI is introduced to decouple kublet from the any runtime.**

## Kubectl (Kube Control)

$`kubectl version`

$`kubectl --help`

$`kubectl get nodes`

- Shows.

$`kubectl run <pod-name> --image=<image-name>` Create a pod directly.

$`kubectl run <pod-name> --image=<image-name> --dry-run=client -o yaml >  <pod-name>.yaml` This does not execute command but returns output in `yaml` file.

$`kubectl create -f <file-name>.yaml` Here, -f stands for file-name. Declarative way to create a POD. Once created this command cannot be used for same pod otherwise exception is thrown at execution of command.

$`kubectl apply -f <file-name>.yaml` Here, -f stands for file-name. Declarative way to create or update a POD. Prefer to use `apply` instead of `create` command.

$`kubectl get pods` List of running POD(s)

$`kubectl describe pod <pod-name>` In-depth description of the specified POD.

$`kubectl delete pod <pod-name>` Delete the specified POD.

